"""
Standalone entry point for behave_worker.exe - a genuinely SEPARATE
executable from local_agent.exe, whose only job is running Behave in its
own fresh, isolated OS process. This file (and behave_worker.spec, which
builds it) exist specifically to fix a real, recurring problem: see the
"why a separate exe" section below.

Usage - this is never run directly by a person; local_agent.exe's own
runner/run_tests.py spawns it as a subprocess, passing it Behave's own
CLI arguments unchanged:

    behave_worker.exe <features_dir> --no-capture --format json --outfile <path>

WHY A SEPARATE EXE (the short version - see runner/run_tests.py's
module docstring for the full history):

Running a test needs a real, separate OS process for isolation (reusing
one long-lived Python process to run Behave repeatedly was tried first
and leaked internal state across runs - a second run failed with
"AmbiguousStep... already defined" even after resetting the documented
step registry). A frozen build has no system python.exe to shell out to
for that separate process the way the non-frozen/server-side path does.

The first fix had local_agent.exe launch A SECOND COPY OF ITSELF with a
hidden --behave-worker flag - which worked, but is exactly the pattern
antivirus/EDR software watches for (a process launching a byte-for-byte
copy of itself is how many self-replicating threats behave). On at
least one real deployment this triggered a PERSISTENT "[WinError 5]
Access is denied" that automatic retries couldn't work around (unlike
the transient "AV briefly locked the file mid-scan" case, which retrying
does fix - see run_behave_cli). A parent app launching a genuinely
DISTINCT helper program, instead, is a completely standard, unflagged
pattern - it's how most desktop apps with a multi-process architecture
already work - so that's what this file is.

local_agent.exe's run_behave_cli() tries this exe FIRST, falling back to
the old self-respawn approach only if this one isn't found sitting
alongside it (see that function) - so an older install that hasn't been
rebuilt with this worker yet keeps working exactly as it did before,
just without this fix, rather than breaking outright.
"""
import sys

from behave.__main__ import main as behave_main

if __name__ == "__main__":
    returncode = behave_main(sys.argv[1:])
    sys.exit(returncode if isinstance(returncode, int) else 0)
